    "K_t": "Evidence:\n[1] Approximately 15,800 were produced.\n[2] The Mauser 13 mm anti-tank rifle (, usually abbreviated T-Gewehr) was the world's first anti-tank rifle—the first rifle designed for the sole purpose of destroying armored targets—and the only anti-tank rifle to see service in World War I.\n[3] Anti-tank warfare arose as a result of the need to develop technology and tactics to destroy tanks during World War I.\n[4] The Type I rifle \"Arisaka\" (イ式小銃 , \"I-shiki shōjū\" ) was produced during the early years of World War II for the Japanese Empire by the Kingdom of Italy (\"Type I\" is not a numeric symbol, it denominates \"Italian\")."
